insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('424a7571-d6d7-562f-ad2e-e8ae4ed77705', 'rg5-word-parts-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-word-parts'), null,
        '3-5', $q$A Hidden World in a Drop of Water$q$, $q$A single drop of pond water looks clear and empty. But a biologist, a scientist who studies living things, knows better. That drop may hold hundreds of tiny creatures.

Most of these creatures are invisible to the human eye. To see them, scientists use a microscope. This tool uses lenses to make small things look many times larger. The word comes from two Greek parts: micro, meaning "small," and scope, meaning "look at."

Under the lens, the drop comes alive. Some creatures are shaped like slippers and swim with tiny hairs. Others look like green balls that roll slowly through the water. Numerous others dart about too fast to follow.

Scientists write careful notes about what they see. Each observation includes the size, shape, and movement of a creature. Over many years, these notes have helped scientists learn how pond life changes with the seasons.

You do not need a fancy lab to try this. Many schools have simple microscopes. With a little patience, you can explore a whole hidden world.$q$, 'draft')
