-- Passage rg6-argument-evidence-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('6bafcfc1-8262-5700-92b4-2393600078f1', 'rg6-argument-evidence-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Smaller Fields for Young Soccer Players$q$, $q$In the Westbrook youth soccer league, eight-year-olds play on the same full-size field as high school players. The league should switch to smaller fields and smaller teams for players under ten.

The main reason is that young players would touch the ball more. Coach Amara Lindqvist filmed two games last fall. In a full-field game with eleven players per team, each child touched the ball about twelve times. In a small-field game with seven players per team, each child touched it about thirty times. More touches mean more chances to practice.

A second reason is that small fields keep players active. On a big field, many young players stand far from the ball and wait. On a small field, everyone is close to the action. Several parents said their children came home more tired and happier after small-field games.

A third reason is that smaller fields help players learn to pass. When the field is crowded with too many players, kids often just kick the ball as far as they can. With fewer players and less space, they learn to look for an open teammate.

Small-sided soccer is clearly the best way to play, and every expert in the world agrees with it.

Some families worry that children will not be ready for the big field later. But players can move to larger fields as they grow, just as they move to larger balls. Learning skills first will make that change easier.

Westbrook should give its youngest players a field that fits them.$q$, 'draft')
