-- Passage rg6-argument-evidence-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('83c2be5a-e039-5bbd-84a7-1cf96488553f', 'rg6-argument-evidence-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Lend Instruments, Grow the Band$q$, $q$At Riverside Middle School, about sixty students play in the band. Many more say they would like to join but cannot. The school should create an instrument lending program so that any student can borrow an instrument for the year.

The biggest reason is cost. A new clarinet or trumpet can cost hundreds of dollars, and renting one can cost more than a family spends on school supplies for the whole year. In a survey of sixth graders, forty students said they wanted to play an instrument. Twenty-six of them said cost was the main thing stopping them.

A lending program would also make good use of instruments that already exist. Many families have old instruments sitting in closets. The band director, Mr. Okonjo, asked families to donate last spring. Within a month, people had given eleven instruments, including two flutes and a trombone.

Playing music also helps students in other ways. Band members learn to practice, listen to others and work toward a concert date. Band is simply the best class at Riverside, and everyone who joins loves it.

Some people worry that borrowed instruments could be damaged. Students could sign a care agreement, and the band could hold a short lesson on cleaning and storing each instrument. A small repair fund, raised at the spring concert, could cover any accidents.

Music should not belong only to students whose families can afford it. A lending program would open the band room door to everyone.$q$, 'draft')
