-- Passage rg6-argument-evidence-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('285b6af8-886f-5a81-bd52-7ac4b44c20fb', 'rg6-argument-evidence-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Bridge Debate of Fairhaven$q$, $q$In 1854, the small farming town of Fairhaven sat on the east side of the Calder River. The nearest market town was on the west side. Farmers had to pay a ferry operator to carry their wagons across, and in spring the river often ran too high for the ferry to cross at all.

A group of farmers, led by a woman named Hester Galloway, asked the town council to build a wooden bridge. They gave several reasons. First, the ferry fee took a large share of each farmer's earnings. Galloway brought a ledger showing that her own family had paid the ferry more than forty times in one year. Second, the ferry had not run for nineteen days the previous April because of high water. During that time, farmers could not sell their milk and eggs, and much of it spoiled.

The ferry operator, Tobias Wren, spoke against the bridge. He said a bridge would surely wash away in the first flood. He also said that "Fairhaven has always used the ferry, and it has always been good enough." Wren did not give examples of other bridges that had failed.

The council asked a builder from a nearby town to study the river. The builder reported that a bridge set on stone piers, high above the spring water line, could stand for many years. He showed drawings of a similar bridge he had built upstream ten years earlier, which was still standing.

The council voted to build the bridge. It opened in 1856 and carried wagons across the Calder for more than fifty years.$q$, 'draft')
