-- Passage rg6-authors-purpose-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('649e7613-7c4a-5146-9677-91d7765e8225', 'rg6-authors-purpose-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Weather Watchers$q$, $q$Long before satellites and computer models, people who wanted to understand the weather needed records. A single day of rain tells you very little. Many years of daily notes, taken in many places, can show patterns. In the 1800s, much of this work was done by volunteers.

These weather watchers were farmers, teachers, shopkeepers, and even students. Each one received a thermometer and a rain gauge, which is a tube that collects and measures rainfall. Every day, at about the same time, the volunteer checked both tools and wrote the numbers in a notebook. At the end of each month, the notebook pages were mailed to a central office.

Picture a farmer stepping outside at dawn in the middle of winter. Snow is falling, and her fingers are stiff with cold. She still brushes off the gauge, reads the thermometer, and writes down every number. She does this again the next day, and the next, for years. She is not paid for her effort.

The work was not exciting, and few people thanked the volunteers. Yet their careful records turned out to be priceless. Scientists today still study those old notebooks to learn how the climate in a place has changed over a long period of time.

We often celebrate great discoveries made in a single moment. The weather watchers remind us that some of the most important knowledge is built slowly, one ordinary day at a time, by people whose names rarely appear in history books.$q$, 'draft')
