-- Passage rg6-authors-purpose-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2c498fbb-b40d-5bb2-a324-b5aac1b82a30', 'rg6-authors-purpose-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Shade for the Bus Stops$q$, $q$Every summer afternoon, people wait for the bus at the corner of Harmon Avenue and Fifth Street. There is a bench, a sign, and a trash can. There is no shade at all. On a hot day, the sidewalk there feels like the inside of an oven. I think the city should plant trees beside every bus stop that has no cover, starting with this one.

Trees do more than look nice. A large shade tree can make the ground beneath it many degrees cooler than open pavement. For an older person or a parent holding a small child, those degrees matter. Riders who feel comfortable are also more likely to keep taking the bus instead of driving.

The city's planning office has said that trees cost money to plant and water, and that falling leaves create extra work for street crews. Those points are true. But a young tree needs regular watering for only its first few years. After that, it can shade the stop for decades. Raking leaves a few weeks each fall is a small price for many summers of comfort.

Some people suggest building metal roofs over the benches instead. A roof would help with rain, but metal heats up in the sun, and it does nothing to cool the air. A tree does both.

Our city calls itself a green place to live. Let's prove it, one bus stop at a time. Please contact the planning office this month and ask for trees at Harmon and Fifth.$q$, 'draft')
