values ('8dd5f7ad-5927-5f1d-9286-fa9ea3fc0652', 'rg6-character-setting-plot-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Wrong Drawer$q$, $q$Every Saturday, Rafael Quispe volunteered in the basement of the Granite Falls Natural History Museum. His job was dull but important. He copied the labels from old wooden drawers of fossils into the museum's new computer catalog. The basement smelled of dust and floor polish, and the only sound was the hum of a fan.

One morning he opened a drawer marked "Horse teeth, collected 1932." The first tooth was flat and ridged, like the horse teeth in his guidebook. The second was not. It was long and curved, with tiny saw-like edges along one side.

Rafael frowned. He was only a volunteer, and the label had been written by a real scientist almost a hundred years ago. He typed "horse tooth" into the catalog and moved on.

But the curved tooth bothered him all through lunch. He pulled out his guidebook and compared pictures. The edges looked just like the teeth of a meat eater, maybe an ancient cat.

After lunch, he carried the drawer upstairs to Dr. Nkemelu, the head of collections. His voice shook a little as he explained. She held the tooth under a magnifying lamp for a long time.

"You're right," she finally said. "This belongs to a sabertooth cat. Someone sorted it into the wrong drawer decades ago."

That afternoon, Dr. Nkemelu asked Rafael to check every drawer from the 1932 collection, not just copy the labels. Rafael went back down to the basement, but he didn't hurry anymore. He looked closely at each fossil before he read its label.$q$, 'draft')
