insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('dbe62968-0873-5568-b7bd-a826d37e3034', 'rg6-comparing-texts-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-comparing-texts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Counting Stars$q$, $q$Text 1: The Night Sky at Camp (a story)

Amira had lived in the city her whole life. From her apartment window, she could see maybe five stars on a clear night, and sometimes one of them turned out to be an airplane.

On the first night of the class camping trip, the counselor, Mr. Dube, led everyone to a dark field and told them to lie on their backs. Amira looked up and gasped. The sky was crowded with stars, thousands of them. A pale, cloudy band stretched from one side to the other.

"That is our own galaxy," Mr. Dube said. "You are seeing it from the inside."

"Were all these stars here the whole time?" Amira asked.

"Every night," he said. "The city lights just hide them."

Amira lay awake long after the others fell asleep. She did not want to close her eyes and lose even one of them.

Text 2: My Three Stars (a poem)

From the roof of our building
I can find only three.
The streetlights burn orange
as far as I see.

My cousin out west
says her sky is a sea.
She counts them by thousands.
I count them by three.

But I know their names now.
I wait for them each night.
Three small friends shining
through all of that light.

Maybe one day I will see
her sky full and wide.
Till then, I keep my three
like coins held inside.$q$, 'draft')
