-- Passage rg6-connections-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e16ac0f4-101f-503e-b025-ed1d5d4d6b15', 'rg6-connections-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-connections'), null,
        '6-8', $q$How an Octopus Disappears$q$, $q$An octopus has no shell, no sharp spines, and no hard skeleton. Its soft body would make an easy meal for sharks, eels, and large fish. Yet octopuses survive in oceans all over the world. Their main defense is an amazing skill: they can change the color and texture of their skin in less than a second.

The secret lies in tiny cells just under the skin called chromatophores. Each one is a small sac of colored pigment, such as red, yellow, or brown. Muscles surround every sac. When the muscles pull, the sac stretches wide and its color shows. When the muscles relax, the sac shrinks to a dot and the color nearly disappears. An octopus has thousands of these sacs, so it can create patterns of spots, stripes, and shadows.

Color is only part of the trick. An octopus can also raise small bumps on its skin. Resting on a rough rock, it can make its skin look bumpy and grainy. Resting on smooth sand, it can flatten its skin until it looks smooth.

Scientists have watched octopuses use this skill in different ways. Some hide by matching the sea floor around them. Others flash dark colors to startle an attacker, then dart away in a cloud of ink. One kind of octopus even copies the shapes and colors of dangerous animals, such as a striped sea snake.

Researchers are still puzzled by one fact. Most octopuses cannot see color the way people do. How they match their backgrounds so well remains an open question, and scientists continue to study it.$q$, 'draft')
