-- Passage rg6-connections-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('43422afb-d87b-58ae-9a1b-fd1bc4cbcc37', 'rg6-connections-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-connections'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Story Quilt of Willow Creek$q$, $q$When the town of Willow Creek turned one hundred years old, the town council wanted a celebration that everyone could join. A parade would last only an afternoon. A statue would cost too much. Rosa Delgado, a high school art teacher, suggested something different: a quilt that told the town's history.

Rosa's plan was to divide the quilt into one hundred squares, one for each year. Any resident could sign up to design a square. Each square would show something that happened in Willow Creek during its year, such as the opening of the first library or the great flood that closed Main Street.

At first, few people signed up. Many said they did not know how to sew. Rosa responded by holding free Saturday workshops in the library basement. She taught simple stitches and showed how to cut shapes from scraps of old clothing. Slowly, the workshops filled with grandparents, teenagers, and even a few small children.

The project also sent people searching for stories. To design their squares, residents read old newspapers and interviewed older neighbors. One student learned that her great-grandfather had helped build the town's first bridge. She stitched that bridge in bright blue thread.

After eighteen months, the hundred squares were sewn together. The finished quilt now hangs in the town hall. Visitors often stand before it for a long time, reading the town's past one square at a time. Rosa likes to say that the quilt is not really about fabric. To her, it is about neighbors learning their shared history together.$q$, 'draft')
