-- Passage rg6-connections-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('78322d29-3f17-5172-a06f-a8691360af2e', 'rg6-connections-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-connections'), null,
        '6-8', $q$How Salmon Feed a Forest$q$, $q$It seems strange to say that fish help trees grow. Yet in the forests along certain northern rivers, that is exactly what happens. The connection begins far away, in the ocean.

Salmon hatch in freshwater streams, then swim out to sea. They spend several years in the ocean, eating and growing large. Their bodies fill with nutrients, such as nitrogen, that come from ocean food. Finally, the adult salmon swim back up the same rivers where they hatched. There, they lay eggs and die.

At this point, the forest takes over. Bears catch salmon in the shallow water and carry them into the woods to eat. A bear often eats only the best parts and leaves the rest on the forest floor. Eagles, ravens, and foxes pick at the leftovers. Insects and tiny living things break down whatever remains.

As the fish break down, their nutrients seep into the soil. Tree roots absorb them. Scientists have tested trees along salmon rivers and found ocean nitrogen inside their wood. Some studies suggest that trees near these rivers grow faster than similar trees along rivers without salmon.

The trees then return the favor. Their shade keeps the streams cool, which young salmon need. Their roots hold the riverbanks in place, so mud does not bury the eggs. When trees fall into the water, they create calm pools where young fish can hide.

This cycle shows how closely living things are linked. When salmon numbers fall, the forest may suffer as well. A weaker forest, in turn, makes the rivers harder places for young salmon to survive.$q$, 'draft')
