-- Passage rg6-context-clues-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('86e3169b-1b47-55bb-a696-cd1f8a911efd', 'rg6-context-clues-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-context-clues'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Mountains of Moving Sand$q$, $q$In the driest deserts on Earth, the land is arid, meaning it receives less than ten inches of rain in a year. Few plants can grow there, so nothing holds the loose sand in place. When the wind blows, it lifts grains of sand and carries them along the ground.

Over time, these grains pile up into hills called dunes. Some dunes are small enough to step over. Others tower hundreds of feet above the desert floor, as tall as a skyscraper.

Dunes do not stay in one spot. They migrate across the desert, moving a few feet or even many yards each year. The wind pushes sand up the gentle side of a dune. At the top, the sand spills down the steep side, so the whole hill slowly creeps forward.

This movement is gradual. A person watching a dune for an afternoon would not notice any change at all. Yet after ten years, the same dune might sit far from where it started.

Moving dunes can cause problems for people who live nearby. They can bury roads, fill wells, and cover fields. To protect their towns, some communities plant tough grasses and shrubs along the edges of the dunes. The roots of these plants grip the sand and stabilize it, so the wind can no longer carry it away. Other towns build fences of woven branches that slow the wind before it reaches the sand.

Scientists study dunes using photos taken from satellites. By comparing pictures from different years, they can measure how far each dune has traveled.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
