-- Passage rg6-context-clues-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('51d8dfb9-ead2-5b85-b7d0-725d6669dde5', 'rg6-context-clues-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-context-clues'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Last Chair$q$, $q$When Ms. Okafor handed out the seating chart for the school orchestra, Leila Haddad found her name at the very bottom of the cello section. Last chair. She stared at the paper and felt her cheeks grow warm.

At home that night, she was reluctant to open her cello case. She wanted to practice about as much as she wanted to clean the bathroom. Her little brother, Sami, poked his head into her room. "Aren't you going to play?" he asked. Leila shrugged and kept looking at photos on the family tablet.

The next afternoon, Leila stayed after rehearsal. She asked Ms. Okafor how the first chair cellist, Tomas, had become so good.

"Tomas was not always first," Ms. Okafor said. "Two years ago, he struggled with the same scales you are learning. He did not quit when they got hard. He persevered, practicing twenty minutes every single day, even on weekends."

That week, Leila tried the same plan. At first her notes sounded dissonant, harsh and clashing instead of smooth. Sami covered his ears and pretended to faint on the couch. But by Friday, the sour notes came less often.

A month later, the orchestra held a chair test. Leila did not play a flawless piece. She missed one note in the middle and rushed the ending. Still, she moved up three seats.

On the bus home, she opened her music folder and counted the pages she still needed to learn. There were dozens. Somehow, the number did not seem daunting anymore. It just seemed like a list of things to do next.$q$, 'draft')
