-- Passage rg6-evidence-inference-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f472f129-e4eb-5ce8-af7a-00ea7c98fa7f', 'rg6-evidence-inference-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Harvesting Salt from the Sea$q$, $q$Along some warm, flat coastlines, workers make salt with help from the sun and the wind. The process uses a series of shallow ponds. Seawater flows from one pond to the next over many months. Each step leaves the water saltier than before.

The first ponds hold water that looks much like the ocean. As the sun heats the surface, some of the water evaporates into the air. The salt stays behind, so the water that remains becomes more concentrated. Workers then open small gates and let this saltier water move into the next pond.

The ponds often change color along the way. Tiny living things called algae grow in the very salty water, and some of them turn the ponds pink or orange. Visitors flying overhead sometimes mistake the bright ponds for paint spills. To the workers, though, the colors are a helpful sign. A deep pink pond usually means the water is almost ready.

In the final ponds, salt crystals form on the bottom and along the edges. Workers wait for a dry stretch of weather before they gather the crystals. A heavy rain at the wrong time can dissolve days of progress. For that reason, many salt farms are found in places with long, dry summers.

After harvesting, the salt is washed, dried and sorted by size. Some of it is sold for cooking. Much more is used to melt ice on winter roads or to make other products. The method is very old, but it still works because it needs little more than seawater, sunshine and patience.$q$, 'draft')
