-- Passage rg6-evidence-inference-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('93912662-8468-5a0e-8e1f-819ac3868486', 'rg6-evidence-inference-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Telling Time Before Clocks$q$, $q$Long ago, people had no watches or phones to tell them the time. Still, they needed to know when to plant crops, when to meet and when to rest. Over thousands of years, people in many parts of the world invented clever tools to measure the passing day.

One of the earliest tools was the sundial. A sundial has a flat face and an upright piece that casts a shadow. As the sun moves across the sky, the shadow moves too, pointing to marks on the face. A sundial works well on a clear afternoon. It is useless at night, however, and it struggles on cloudy days.

To solve that problem, people built water clocks. A simple water clock is a container with a tiny hole near the bottom. Water drips out at a steady rate, and lines on the inside show how much time has passed as the level drops. Some water clocks were used to time speeches or meetings, so that each speaker received a fair turn.

Water clocks had weaknesses of their own. As the water level dropped, there was less weight pushing down, so the water dripped more slowly. Makers learned to shape their containers wider at the top and narrower at the bottom to balance this effect. In cold places, the water could also freeze.

Other inventors tried candles marked with lines and glass timers filled with sand. Each tool fixed one problem but created another. Only when mechanical clocks with gears and weights appeared did people have a steady way to measure time day and night, in any weather.$q$, 'draft')
