   $q$Which conclusion about early timekeeping tools is best supported by the passage as a whole?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The sundial was the most accurate timekeeping tool that people ever made.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"People did not care about knowing the time until mechanical clocks appeared.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Water clocks quickly replaced every other tool and were used everywhere.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overstates-evidence"},{"text":"Each tool had limits, so people kept inventing better ways to measure time.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Each tool had limits, so people kept inventing better ways to measure time.$q$, $q$The passage moves from sundials to water clocks to candles and sand timers, noting that "each tool fixed one problem but created another." Paragraph 1 says people needed to know the time long ago, so they did care before mechanical clocks.$q$, 'draft', '93912662-8468-5a0e-8e1f-819ac3868486')
